45498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186058889 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a59f56e-4565-51e2-8e93-bdc2b248a059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059519 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059519 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059519 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "935e8644-8d66-5be6-87ec-b8176675aa91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059519 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 32 = \\dfrac x2\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n![](images/8e/8e7b2d027a761ba93f679cfb517709811938da412b06e59212391741b5660a2b.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059520 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059520 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059520 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7d11d09-773c-58a9-8582-2cd47a3b141c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059520 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x+1}{1} = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\dfrac 12 .\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n![](images/d7/d7150c6093121f9f6dc0ecd9675d5be8f0855d339f3404d7a357875362d9990f.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059521 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059521 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059521 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ae175a8-7654-57c8-b5d8-33d7892b7a24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059521 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x-2}{1} = \\dfrac{5x}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to \n\n$$\nx-2 = \\dfrac{5x}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (x-2) & =3\\cdot \\frac{5x}{3} \\\\ 3x-6 & =5x \\\\ 3x-5x & =6 \\\\ -2x & =6 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059522 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059522 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13f9fd01-802e-5331-b422-b9aa2cdda7ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059522 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=\\dfrac 12$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n\n- $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{2x-1}{1} = \\dfrac{x+1}1\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n2x-1 = x+1.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =x+1 \\\\ 2x-x & =1+1 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059706 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059706 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059706 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059706 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059706 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059706 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Rational Equations with a Variable in One Denominator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059706 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059706 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4af25d77-e2aa-585a-971f-693c44f34e48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059707 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059707 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059707 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059707 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059707 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059707 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Rational Equations with a Binomial in One Denominator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059707 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059707 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "93665eb6-5d3e-52d2-afc6-bc27488190e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059708 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048725 #edb/ref 17592