g Rational Equations Using the Flip Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059941 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186060674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059941 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059941 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059941 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "780b6d06-4e3c-5fb9-a687-1ad2687e0771" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060370 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060370 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060370 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{x} = \\frac{1}{2}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060370 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb4b681b-2ab2-5879-bf11-cb5badc0bb00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060374 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060374 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060374 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x-3}=\\frac{2}{x+6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060374 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b4e7858-1264-5def-86e5-7598a8adf5a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060374 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{x+6}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $6.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{3x+18}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{3x+18}{6} \\\\ x-3 & =3x+18 \\\\ x-3x & =18+3 \\\\ -2x & =21 \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-2x) & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)21 \\\\ (-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})(-\\cancel{2}x) & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)21 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186576411] [17592186060376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060376 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060376 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060376 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3x+2}=-\\frac{4}{x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09320d7a-d867-531b-9740-66504912149d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060376 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{2}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+4}{4}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{6x+4}{4} & =4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{4}\\right) \\\\ 6x+4 & =-x \\\\ 6x+x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186576411] [17592186060377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060377 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060377 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060377 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{1}{x+1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6bbc3969-7e62-5072-9fb8-19f0fe79bb27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060377 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{x+1}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}=x+1\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & =x+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060580 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060580 #edb/ref 1061 15458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060580 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a0e30d07-9c41-5c2c-839c-9e49ca64764c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060580 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060581 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060581 #edb/ref 1061 15459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060581 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "233132be-0fc4-52d1-9152-818b8c45b168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060581 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060582 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060582 #edb/ref 1061 15460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060582 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13e292aa-c881-5526-9904-6d174bbb856b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060582 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060583 #edb/ref 1061 15461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060583 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "634f203a-900b-5f9f-977b-e1bb2ab2c5ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060583 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060674 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060674 #edb/ref 1061 17016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060674 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b043ea3a-fd3a-5de4-b7ac-21a29627ca53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060674 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060937 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that to solve a rational equation, such as the one below, we can use cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot 2 & =3\\cdot x \\\\ 10 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is also another method that we can use, known as the **flip method,** which involves taking the reciprocal of both sides of an equation. So, when an equation involves two fractions, we can \"flip\" them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nThe flip method is useful because it allows us to remove a variable from a denominator. For example, applying the flip method, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}x}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}x}{\\color{red}2} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the resulting equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cancel{2}} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060937 #edb/ref 1065 3123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060937 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "33bd96be-8ef8-525c-be19-8590b4893b52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060937 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165866 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165866 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165866 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8cbb5c90-610e-5a65-8619-e17d9a570b7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165867 #edb/ref 1080 "0.2691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165867 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165867 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b0357732-7d25-5b9d-85e1-5cab3acc7ecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165868 #edb/ref 1080 "0.2249" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165868 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165868 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b8dae3a7-91fb-52c0-b0bc-cdbf28927294" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165869 #edb/ref 1080 "0.2401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165869 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165869 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0aeeb7c9-2abe-518f-b9b1-a59adb1fe821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165870 #edb/ref 1080 "0.1547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165870 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165870 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6c2be036-5f5e-5179-889b-f45060731336" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165871 #edb/ref 1080 "0.1804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165871 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186165871 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "715aef0b-7e80-505a-90f3-c0bb91180806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166654 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166654 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(4)$, compute $P(X = 2)$, rounded to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166654 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fba5b411-86fe-5d2f-85ee-87cfa1e96d83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166654 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(4)$, so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{4^x e^{-4}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 2) &= f(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4^{2}e^{-4}}{2!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1465\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166655 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8466" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166655 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(5)$, compute $P(X < 3)$, rounded to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166655 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62d1e798-9a57-5657-9a6d-8fb70bff0ccd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166655 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(5)$, so the probability distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{5^x e^{-5}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 3) &= P(X ∈ {0, 1, 2}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 0) + P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) \\\\\n&= f(0) + f(1) + f(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5^{0}e^{-5}}{0!} + \\frac{5^{1}e^{-5}}{1!} + \\frac{5^{2}e^{-5}}{2!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1247\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166656 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166656 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(3)$, compute $P(2 < X < 5)$, rounded to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166656 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c89b3081-38d7-5430-b912-82f84ef532ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166656 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(3)$, so the probability distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(2 < X < 5) &= P(X ∈ {3, 4}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 3) + P(X = 4) \\\\\n&= f(3) + f(4) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3^{3}e^{-3}}{3!} + \\frac{3^{4}e^{-3}}{4!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.3921\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166657 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166657 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(3)$, compute $P(X > 2)$, rounded to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166657 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1e693a2-073f-5f8c-98fd-3b89496521d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166657 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}\n$$\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(3)$, so the probability distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X > 2$. However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 2) &= 1 - P(X \\le 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 2) &= P(X ∈ {0, 1, 2}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 0) + P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) \\\\\n&= f(0) + f(1) + f(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3^{0}e^{-3}}{0!} + \\frac{3^{1}e^{-3}}{1!} + \\frac{3^{2}e^{-3}}{2!} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.4232\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 2) &= 1 - P(X \\le 2) \\\\\n&\\approx 1 - 0.4232 \\\\\n&= 0.5768\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166816 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166816 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166816 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166816 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166816 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167484 #edb/ref 17592186045